=====================

 S~S TruSoft Computing LLC (TSC LLC) Repository Philosophy

------------------------------------------

 S~S   SimpleSal explains the managed object concept used a basis for this repository

    This is the README about the managed objects in this repo.

    TSC LLC's idea of a managed object excludes anything present in the repo that is metadata.

    The repo database contains what TSC LLC calls the "repo object": that thing the repo controls.
        The repo database MANAGER applications maintain metadata about and outside the repo object.

    A "managed object" is what TSC LLC calls everything in the repo, EXCEPT files ABOUT the repo.
        The repo database OWNER maintains metadata about the managed object.
        The repo database OWNER invents conventions to help separate managed object from METADATA.

    TSC LLC's chosen publishing style:
        1) the repo contains human metadata text files and the managed object as defined here;
        2) naming conventions followed for separating metadata are adopted (GiTHUB and LiNUX);
        3) the managed object is a single subdirectory in the root of the repository.

    Note: this is not conventional; it may result in metadata files in the App directory.
        The convention for many projects is that metadata files co-exist with the software files.

------------------------------------------

=====================

 S~S  SimpleSal Ecosystem metadata general content guide

------------------------------------------

    When a repository user is looking at a repository for the first time, they see the root of
    the repository.  In the root are files describing the managed object within the repository,
    each of those files is an information signal from the publisher of the managed object ABOUT
    the managed object.  The software publishing culture uses common signals to communicate.

    The signals are "about" the managed object's data so they are called "metadata":
        1) version control (RELEASE, CHANGELOG);
        2) licensing (LiCENSE, COPYiNG);
        3) README.

    README is the window or lens through which the other files make sense.  At some point back
    in computer history, someone made some notes about "it" for themselves, and then for others.
    Before that time, humans only communicated through their voice, so they included a HEARME.

    The consumer of the notes will be new to the content, having forgotten, or been given, a copy.

    When the software you publish changes, or is adopted for use by others, other files follow.
    Because SimpleSal is an introduction to the moderately complex, sophistication has to wait.

------------------------------------------

 S~S  Publishing a SimpleSal App

  TSC LLC is aware of the wise saying "be careful what you wish for, you just might get it".

  The intention of the design of SimpleSal is to enable developers to write simple solutions
  to common problems, based on the passage of Time and the exchange of signals through a medium.

  The intention of the repository collection under the username simplesal on GitHub is
  to provide a medium through which information about software development can be exchanged.

  A proper (e.g., copyleft-licensed) SimpleSal App from any developer is proof it is working.

  There will be a mechanism through which developers submit SimpleSal Apps for posting here.

  It could turn out to be 5 SimpleSal Apps, it could be 500; the repository is ready for it.

------------------------------------------

 S~S  Making modifications to SimpleSal

  A design goal: no modifications to SimpleSal files are needed to modify the behavior or content.

  Modifying an App and SimpleSal at the same time is complex AND ALSO what SimpleSal is made for.

    Modifying the configuration of ssTEA is expected; this is about modifying SimpleSal software.

  Nothing in the repository organization blocks having modified versions of both at the same time.

------------------------------------------

=====================

 S~S This Repository

------------------------------------------

 S~S  What is the purpose of the software in this repository?

    * instantiate a complete system of Time Keeping and Event Management within an Arduino App.
    * Deliver the SimpleSal software as a self-contained directory appropriate to all use cases.
    * Support SimpleSal ecosystem educational goals by providing free software source code.
    * Provide software of moderate complexity that can be broken, fixed, improved, abandoned.

------------------------------------------

 S~S  What happens in my workspace as a result of interacting with this repository?

  * for the SimpleSal source:

      the managed object, a directory, goes into the "\App" directory as "\App\SimpleSal\".

      A repository-to-instance transfer may include the SimpleSal repo metadata files in the
      App directory, depending upon the method of distribution of the SimpleSal repo contents.

  The SimpleSal App references the version of SimpleSal indicated by the software build.

      This means the include path used by the software build determines where rr\SimpleSal lives.
      The SimpleSal model using the Arduino out-of-the-box build environment is rr\App\SimpleSal.

      A more sophisticated user, with for example multiple SimpleSal Apps, could use one copy.

    The contents of the App's directory and the contents of rr\SimpleSal are mutually exclusive.

------------------------------------------

=====================

 S~S   directory tree structure:

------------------------------------------

  Model: user space is a directory "App" containing the simplesal directory and "App.ino".

  The Arduino IDE build environment expects a SimpleSal directory to be in each App's directory.

  the \App\SimpleSal\ directory contains all of the software source code needed to use SimpleSal.

    the ssTEA subdirectory contains the software implementing Time and Event Agency (TEA).

    the ssUI subdirectory contains the menu system that displays Time and Event information.

    the ssDocs subdirectory contains documentation, each file is pointed to by source code files.

    the ssIO subdirectory is independent of ssTEA and implements the virtualization of hardware.

------------------------------------------

=====================

 S~S  TruSoft Computing defends the use of the C language one time, here at the front door.

------------------------------------------

  C language software at a "fundamental Kernighan and Ritchie C level" is also called "K and R C".
    The primary higher-level concept used from K&R C by SimpleSal is pointers to software.
    The actual use of a code-space pointer is not as important as the concept of a pointer to code.

    Any software known to the compiler by name can be referenced by name, to get the pointer value.
    Operating systems are not interested in the name of an interrupt handler, just a pointer to it.

  C software survives because it is compilable into processor-level instructions and capabilities;
    material within the documentation maps C algorithms to the resulting ARM assembly language.
    What some viewers may find particularly interesting is how code pointers work in ARM processors.

  SimpleSal is also going to be integrated into other languages and Hosts; we must begin at simple
    if we want to extend support to higher-level languages (HLLs); they each have methods to access
    C-based software from the HLL; within constraints imposed by their own architecture.

    Because TSC LLC knows that higher-level languages eventually execute the same 50 instructions in
    the processor, the task of extending support to a higher-level language is a function of demand.

------------------------------------------

=====================

 S~S   Documentation is distributed across the ecosystem relative to the topic

------------------------------------------

  There is ssDocs documentation specific to the architecture and implementation of ssTEA and ssUI,
  as software components co-existing with the App.  For example, allocation of the database used
  for Event descriptors may be performed by the App or ssTEA; the App is free to take control and
  replace ssTEA's static allocation algorithm with a dynamic allocation using malloc.  Try it.

